i n\t private\t\nuniversities/colleges\tin\tthe\tcountry;\t\n(b)\tif\tso,\tthe\tdetails\tthereof;\tand\t\n(c)\tthe\tsteps\ttaken\tby\tthe\tGovernment/University\tGrants\tCommiss ion\tto\tdeal\twith\tthe\t\nemerging\tchallenges\tin\tthe\tfield\tof\thigher\teducation?\t\nANSWER\t\t\nMINISTER\tOF\tHUMAN\tRESOURCE\tDEVELOPMENT\t\n(SHRI\n\tRAMESH\tPOKHRIYAL\t‘NISHANK’)\t\n\t\n(a):\t No\t Sir.\t Presently,\t Private\t Universities\t are\t regulated\t by\t U niversity\t Grants\t\nCommission\t(UGC)\tas\tper\tthe\tprovisions\tof\tthe\tUGC\t(Establishmen t\tof\tand\tMaintenance\t\nof\tStandards\tin\tPrivate\tUniversities)\tRegulations,\t2003.\t\n(b):\tIn\tview\tof\tthe\t(a)\tabove,\tdoes\tnot\tarise.\t\t\n( c ) : \tI n \to r d e r \tt o \td e a l \tw i t h \tt h e \te m e r g i n g \tc h a l l e n g e s , \tt h e \tG o v e r n ment\t /\t UGC\t has\t taken\t\ns e v e r a l \tm e a s u r e s \tw h i c h \ti n c l u d e \tp r o m o t i o n \to f \ta u t o n o m y \tt o \tH i g h e r \tEducational\t\nInstitutions,\tencouraging\tinstitutions\tto\timprove\tthe\tquality\tof\tresearch\tthrough\tvarious\t\nschemes\t viz.\t Impacting\t Research\t Innovation\t and\t Technology\t(IMPR INT),\t Impactful\t\nPolicy\t Research\t in\tSocial\t Sciences\t(IMPRESS),\tGlobal\tInitiative\tfor\tAcademics\tNetwork\t\n(GIAN),\t Study\t Webs\t of\t Active\t Learning\t for\t Young\t Aspiring\t Minds\t (SWAYAM),\t Staff\t\nTraining\t and\t Research\t Institute\t of\t Distance\t Education\t (STRIDE),\t etc.\t The\t focus\t is\t on\t\nL e a r n i n g \tO u t c o m e \tb a s e d \tC u r r i c u l u m \t( L O C F ) \tt o \tm a k e \tl e a r n i n g \to r i e nted\t expectations\t\nand\t outcome\t at\t the\t end\t of\t Under\t Graduate\t /\t Post\t Graduate\t level.\tT h e \tU G C \th a s \ta l s o \t\nbrought\t out\t Regulations\t on\t Promotion\t of\t Academic\t Integrity\t and\t prevention\t of\t\nPlagiarism\t in\t Higher\t Educational\t Institutions\t and\t Regulations\t o n \tO p e n \t& \tD i s t a n c e \t\nLearning\tin\t2018.\t\t\t\n *****\t"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|1|UNSTARRED |336", "t": "Sources of Funds for Maintenance of Monuments", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 336 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nSOURCES OF FUNDS FOR MAINTENENACE OF MONUMENTS \n \n336. DR. (PROF.) KIRIT PREMJIBHAI SOLANKI \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the funds received from PSUs through Corporate Social Responsibility \nare the only source towards the maintenance of historical monuments apart from \nthe funds generated through ticketing and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) if not, the other sources of the funds for the maintenance thereof; and \n \n(c) the percentage contribution of the d ifferent heads through which  such funds \nreceived for maintenance of historical monuments? \n \nANSWER \n \n    MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE AND TOURISM \n \n(PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b) No Sir, the conservation and maintenance work of Centrally Protected \nmonuments is attended from the funds allocated through the budget of the \nGovernment of India. The contribution of Corporate Social Responsibility  is for \ndevelopmental works at selected monuments through NCF  (National Culture Fund). \nThe revenue collected through various sources like Ticketing, Photography, Filming, \nParking, holding Cultural events, etc. at selected centrally protected monument is \nnot utilized by the department for any work and is deposited in the Consolidated \nFund of India. \n \n(c) The details of the funds received from various sources during last three years is \ngiven in Annexure - I. \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure - I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 336 FOR 24.06.2019 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \nFinancial \nYear \nFunds  received from ASI \nbudget for Conservation \nand Maintenance (in lakhs) \nFunds received from NCF (in \nlakhs) \n1 2016-17 30770.76 300  \n2 2017-18 41080.94 250 \n3 2018-19 4067.86 634 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|1|UNSTARRED |337", "t": "High Fees by Coaching Institutes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 337 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 24.06.2019 \n \nHigh Fees by Coaching Institutes \n \n337. DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that  despite increase in incomes, access to \nquality learning is still far from reality and coaching institutes are charging high \nfees to prepare students get admission to Government Institutes like NIT, IIT, \nIIM and AIIMS etc. and if so, the reaction of the Government thereto;  \n(b)  the measures which taken to cap the high fees by the coaching institutes to \nhelp economically weaker students to get access to high quality education; and  \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to introduce some special scheme to \nensure high quality Higher education at  an affordable cost to all including \neconomically weaker students and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI RAMESH POKHRIYAL ‘NISHANK’) \n \n(a) to (c) Private Coaching Institutes do not come under the purview of this \nMinistry.  Many of these operate at the school level and hence are under the direct \npurview of the State Governments. However, this Ministry has issued directions to all \nState Governments to take steps for effective regulation of such centres. In the \ninterest of students including the most disadvantaged, Government has launched \nStudy Webs of Active Learning for Young Aspiring Minds (SWAYAM), which offers \ninteractive course content such as video lectures, reading material, self-assessment \nthrough tests and quizzes and online disc ussion forum for cl earing doubts for \nstudents from Class 9 to post-graduation.  The content can be accessed free of cost \nby anyone, anywhere at any time. Another in itiative of the Government called Indian \nInstitute of Technology - Professor Assisted Learning (IIT-PAL) provides video content \nin Biology, Chemistry, Mathematics and Physics prepared by IIT Professors and \nKendirya Vidyalaya (KV) teachers for Class XI  and Class XII students aspiring to join \nIITs and other Institutions.  The content is available free of cost on dedicated DTH \nchannels of SWAYAM PRABHA. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|1|UNSTARRED |338", "t": "Teaching Standards", "head": " \n \nO.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n338\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n  \n16\nTH\n \nJULY, 2019\n \n \n \n \nINCREASE IN FARMERS’ SUICIDES\n \n \n*338. \n    \nSHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n  \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether it is a fact that there has been an increase in the number of cases of suicides \nby farmers in the country; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the reasons therefor; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nif not, the details thereof during the last five years, year\n-\nwise?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\nc\n):\n \nA statement is\n \nlaid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nc\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n338\n  \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n16\nTH\n \nJULY, 2019\n.\n \n \n(a) \nto (c): \n \nThe National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) under the Ministry of Home \nAffairs compiles and disseminates information on suicides in its publication titled ‘Accidental \nDeaths and Suicides in India’ (ADSI). These Reports on suicides are available upto 2015 on \ni\nts website. The Reports for the year 2016 onwards have not been published. \n \n \nThe total number of suicides by farmers for the years 2014 and 2015 in the country \nState\n-\nwise as per the ADSI reports is \nannexed.\n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \n \nStatement in respect of Part (a) to \n(c) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 338 due for 16.07.2019. \nregarding ‘Increase in Farmers’ Suicides’\n \n \nState/UT wise number of Suicides by Self Employed persons in Farming/Agriculture during 2014\n-\n2015\n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nState / UT\n \n2014\n \n2015\n \nSelf\n-\nemployed \nPersons \n[Agriculture \n(Farmers)]\n \nSelf\n-\nemployed \nPersons \n[Agriculture \n(Agricultural \nLabourers)]\n \nSelf\n-\nemployed \nPersons \n[Agriculture \n(Total)] Sum \nof Col. 1+2\n \nFarmers/\n \nCultivators\n \nAgricultural \nLabourers\n \nSuicides \nin \nFarming \nSector \n(Total) \nSum of \nCol. 4+5\n \n \n \n \n \n1\n \n2\n \n3\n \n4\n \n5\n \n6\n \n1\n \nANDHRA \nPRADESH\n \n160\n \n472\n \n632\n \n516\n \n400\n \n916\n \n2\n \nARUNACHAL \nPRADESH\n \n0\n \n3\n \n3\n \n7\n \n3\n \n10\n \n3\n \nASSAM\n \n21\n \n38\n \n59\n \n84\n \n54\n \n138\n \n4\n \nBIHAR\n \n0\n \n10\n \n10\n \n0\n \n7\n \n7\n \n5\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n443\n \n312\n \n755\n \n854\n \n100\n \n954\n \n6\n \nGOA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n0\n \n7\n \nGUJARAT\n \n45\n \n555\n \n600\n \n57\n \n244\n \n301\n \n8\n \nHARYANA\n \n14\n \n105\n \n119\n \n28\n \n134\n \n162\n \n9\n \nHIMACHAL \nPRADESH\n \n32\n \n31\n \n63\n \n0\n \n46\n \n46\n \n10\n \nJAMMU & \nKASHMIR\n \n12\n \n25\n \n37\n \n0\n \n21\n \n21\n \n11\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n0\n \n4\n \n4\n \n0\n \n21\n \n21\n \n12\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n321\n \n447\n \n768\n \n1197\n \n372\n \n1569\n \n13\n \nKERALA\n \n107\n \n700\n \n807\n \n3\n \n207\n \n210\n \n14\n \nMADHYA \nPRADESH\n \n826\n \n372\n \n1198\n \n581\n \n709\n \n1290\n \n15\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \