te:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to give more importance to Animal Husbandry, Dairy and \nFishery sectors and if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(b) whether it has become necessary to pay more attention towards milch cattle due to irregular \nrain and shortage of water in various parts of the country and if so, the steps proposed to be taken \nby the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n (a)  Yes, Sir.  The Government is implementing various schemes to supplement the efforts of \nStates/UTs for development of animal husbandry and fisheries sectors namely, \n  \nI. Rashtriya Gokul Mission  \nII. National Programme for Dairy Development  \nIII. National Dairy Plan-I \nIV. Dairy Entrepreneurship Development Scheme \nV. Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund \nVI. Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organizations engaged in dairy \nactivities \nVII. National Livestock Mission \nVIII. Livestock Health & Disease Control \nIX. Blue Revolution: Integrated Development and Management of Fisheries \nX. Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund \n  \nIn addition, recently, the Cabinet in its 1 st meeting held on 31.05.2019 has approved an \nambitious scheme “ National Animal Disease Control Programme (NADCP) for control of Foot & \nMouth Disease and Brucellosis” by vaccinating 100% cattle, buffalo, sheep, goat and pig population \nfor FMD and 100% bovine female calves of 4 -8 months of age for brucellosis. The programme has \na total outlay of Rs.13,343.00 crore for five years (2019-20 to 2023-24). \n(b) Yes, Sir. The Government issues advisory to the States/UTs to mitigate drought or drought like \nsituation due to deficit rainfall. The Government has also developed Disaster management plan in \nconsultation with National Disaster Management Authority. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|1|UNSTARRED |433", "t": "Ong